Below is a **stand‑alone HTML file** that contains a minimal, production‑ready JavaScript animation.  
It draws a bouncing ball on an HTML `<canvas>` using `requestAnimationFrame`.  
All the logic lives in a single `<script>` block, so you can copy the file and open it in any modern browser.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Simple Bouncing Ball Animation</title>
  <style>
    /* Center the canvas and give it a subtle border */
    body {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: center;
      align-items: center;
      height: 100vh;
      margin: 0;
      background: #f0f0f0;
    }
    canvas {
      background: #ffffff;
      border: 2px solid #333;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <canvas id="animCanvas" width="600" height="400"></canvas>

  <script>
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // Configuration
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    const config = {
      ballRadius: 20,
      ballColor: '#ff5722',
      gravity: 0.6,          // acceleration (px/frame²)
      friction: 0.98,       // energy loss on bounce (0‑1)
      startX: 100,
      startY: 100,
      startVX: 4,
      startVY: 0
    };

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // Canvas setup
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    const canvas = document.getElementById('animCanvas');
    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // Ball state
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    const ball = {
      x: config.startX,
      y: config.startY,
      vx: config.startVX,
      vy: config.startVY,
      radius: config.ballRadius,
      color: config.ballColor
    };

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // Helper: draw a filled circle
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    function drawBall({ x, y, radius, color }) {
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(x, y, radius, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.fillStyle = color;
      ctx.fill();
      ctx.closePath();
    }

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // Main animation loop
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    function animate() {
      // Clear the canvas
      ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);

      // Apply physics
      ball.vy += config.gravity;          // gravity
      ball.x += ball.vx;
      ball.y += ball.vy;

      // Collision detection – floor
      if (ball.y + ball.radius > canvas.height) {
        ball.y = canvas.height - ball.radius;
        ball.vy *= -config.friction;     // reverse & dampen
        ball.vx *= config.friction;      // optional horizontal friction
      }

      // Collision detection – ceiling
      if (ball.y - ball.radius < 0) {
        ball.y = ball.radius;
        ball.vy *= -config.friction;
      }

      // Collision detection – walls
      if (ball.x + ball.radius > canvas.width) {
        ball.x = canvas.width - ball.radius;
        ball.vx *= -config.friction;
      }
      if (ball.x - ball.radius < 0) {
        ball.x = ball.radius;
        ball.vx *= -config.friction;
      }

      // Render
      drawBall(ball);

      // Queue next frame
      requestAnimationFrame(animate);
    }

    // Kick‑off
    requestAnimationFrame(animate);
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### How it works
1. **Configuration** – All tunable parameters (size, colors, physics) are stored in a single `config` object for easy tweaking.
2. **Physics** – Each frame we add gravity to the vertical velocity, then move the ball. When the ball hits a boundary we invert the velocity and apply a `friction` factor to simulate energy loss.
3. **Rendering** – The canvas is cleared, then the ball is drawn with `arc` and `fill`.
4. **Animation loop** – `requestAnimationFrame` provides a smooth 60 fps loop that automatically throttles when the tab is hidden.

You can drop this file into any web project, adjust the `config` values, or extend the logic (e.g., add multiple balls, user interaction, etc.). Enjoy!

